WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:00.974
- Good morning Believe Nation,

00:00:00.974 --> 00:00:03.532
my name is Evan Carmichael,
my one word is Believe

00:00:03.532 --> 00:00:05.059
and I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:05.059 --> 00:00:07.422
will solve all of the major
problems in the world.

00:00:07.422 --> 00:00:11.044
So today's message is work on yourself.

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:12.931
Over to you, Oprah Winfrey.

00:00:12.931 --> 00:00:15.236
(rooster crowing)

00:00:15.236 --> 00:00:17.641
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:17.641 --> 00:00:20.358
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:20.358 --> 00:00:23.858
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:24.978 --> 00:00:29.145
- I say to my girls all of
the time, that your real work

00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:34.791
is to figure out where your power base is

00:00:36.844 --> 00:00:40.927
and to work on the alignment
of your personality,

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:44.743
your gifts, that you have to give

00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:47.227
with the real reason why you're here,

00:00:47.227 --> 00:00:50.404
that's the number one
thing, you have to do

00:00:50.404 --> 00:00:54.237
is to work on yourself
and to fill yourself up

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:59.584
and keep your cup full,
keep yourself full.

00:00:59.584 --> 00:01:02.965
Now, I used to be afraid of
that, I used to be afraid,

00:01:02.965 --> 00:01:04.108
particularly from people who say,

00:01:04.108 --> 00:01:06.129
"Oh, she's so full of herself,

00:01:06.129 --> 00:01:09.118
"mm, she's so full of herself,"

00:01:09.118 --> 00:01:10.868
and now I embrace it,

00:01:11.942 --> 00:01:16.738
I consider it a compliment,
that I am full of myself,

00:01:16.738 --> 00:01:19.543
because you only when you're full,

00:01:19.543 --> 00:01:22.923
I'm full, I'm overflowing,
my cup runneth over,

00:01:22.923 --> 00:01:25.915
I've so much, I have so much
to offer and so much to give

00:01:25.915 --> 00:01:29.998
and I am not afraid of
honoring myself, you know,

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:34.153
it's miraculous when you think about it.

00:01:34.153 --> 00:01:35.873
- I really love how Oprah Winfrey

00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:37.520
turned what was intended as a negative,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.943
but being full of yourself

00:01:38.943 --> 00:01:41.574
and she turns that into a positive,

00:01:41.574 --> 00:01:44.440
and I think it's such a
necessary skill as entrepreneurs,

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.571
to take negative things and
find the positivity in it,

00:01:47.571 --> 00:01:49.731
to find the optimism in it,
to find the solutions in it,

00:01:49.731 --> 00:01:52.652
'cause that's how we
stand out and succeed.

00:01:52.652 --> 00:01:54.518
I also really agree with
the point on developing

00:01:54.518 --> 00:01:57.450
and working on yourself and
this is working it really hard,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.307
it's easy to say I'm
going to work on myself,

00:01:59.307 --> 00:02:02.175
it's easy to say I'm going to
work on improving this new skill

00:02:02.175 --> 00:02:03.424
or learning that new language

00:02:03.424 --> 00:02:04.592
or building that new business,

00:02:04.592 --> 00:02:06.834
or whatever it is that
you want to go out and do,

00:02:06.834 --> 00:02:09.403
it's easy to say, but
it's hard to execute,

00:02:09.403 --> 00:02:11.046
it's hard to execute because chances are,

00:02:11.046 --> 00:02:13.747
you're the only one in your environment,

00:02:13.747 --> 00:02:14.969
that actually cares about it,

00:02:14.969 --> 00:02:16.478
like say you want to be a YouTuber,

00:02:16.478 --> 00:02:18.519
you look at these YouTubes and
say, "I want to be a YouTuber,

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:20.071
"I want to build a business on YouTube,

00:02:20.071 --> 00:02:21.447
"I want to have a impact,

00:02:21.447 --> 00:02:23.718
"I have a message, that needs to come out,

00:02:23.718 --> 00:02:26.093
"I want to get my
YouTube channel started."

00:02:26.093 --> 00:02:28.051
Chances are, you're the only person,

00:02:28.051 --> 00:02:30.816
that you know, who wants to do YouTube

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:32.351
and so it can be hard to stay motivated,

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:34.614
because the people around you,
some of them may be negative,

00:02:34.614 --> 00:02:36.592
some of them may be saying,
"Well, you can't do that,

00:02:36.592 --> 00:02:37.604
"you've never gone to school for that,

00:02:37.604 --> 00:02:39.550
"what makes you think you
can create a YouTube channel,

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.511
"are you kidding me, do
you know who you are?"

00:02:41.511 --> 00:02:43.524
That's great, some people may be negative,

00:02:43.524 --> 00:02:46.011
some people though may just not care

00:02:46.011 --> 00:02:47.760
and some people may be encouraging,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:50.790
but no matter how encouraging they are,

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.834
you are the one who cares
the most about this dream

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:57.007
and when you don't have
the people around you,

00:02:57.007 --> 00:02:58.582
who are also doing the same thing,

00:02:58.582 --> 00:03:00.482
especially at a higher level than you are,

00:03:00.482 --> 00:03:03.162
it can be really hard to
continue and stay motivated,

00:03:03.162 --> 00:03:04.800
and what happens then is you have a day,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.003
when you're motivated, you're
off, you're working hard,

00:03:07.003 --> 00:03:08.050
you're creating your channel,

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.075
you want to make a new video

00:03:09.075 --> 00:03:11.412
and then you wake up the next day like,

00:03:11.412 --> 00:03:12.637
that momentum has gone,

00:03:12.637 --> 00:03:14.510
you say, "Who am I to make
a video, I'm not very good,

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.626
"did you see what I did
yesterday, that was terrible,"

00:03:17.626 --> 00:03:18.964
and then you get inspired
by something else,

00:03:18.964 --> 00:03:20.688
maybe you watch a video and
then you're on fire again,

00:03:20.688 --> 00:03:22.142
like, "I'm going to make another video,"

00:03:22.142 --> 00:03:25.098
and you go off and you create
it and then you fall off,

00:03:25.098 --> 00:03:28.597
and so this constant up
and down, rollercoaster,

00:03:28.597 --> 00:03:29.872
yo yo, you know, up and down,

00:03:29.872 --> 00:03:32.675
never really getting any consistency in it

00:03:32.675 --> 00:03:33.508
and so the key for me,

00:03:33.508 --> 00:03:35.923
when there's something that
you really want to work on,

00:03:35.923 --> 00:03:38.782
that nobody else in your
environment cares about,

00:03:38.782 --> 00:03:42.151
is putting it into your
calendar and scheduling it out.

00:03:42.151 --> 00:03:43.988
If you goal is to have success on YouTube,

00:03:43.988 --> 00:03:47.433
as an example, or insert
whatever you goal is,

00:03:47.433 --> 00:03:50.666
then schedule it out, make
time for it every single day,

00:03:50.666 --> 00:03:53.054
that you're spending time
building your skills,

00:03:53.054 --> 00:03:56.458
learning the language of that
industry to get better at it,

00:03:56.458 --> 00:03:59.558
honing your craft, because on
the days where you're on fire

00:03:59.558 --> 00:04:02.380
and motivated, you won't need
to schedule, you'll just be,

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.852
you'll blast through it and
just have tons of productivity,

00:04:05.852 --> 00:04:10.045
but on the off days, on the
days in between those spikes,

00:04:10.045 --> 00:04:11.565
the schedule helps you stay committed

00:04:11.565 --> 00:04:13.320
to the goal that you have,

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.341
the schedule helps you stay on path,

00:04:15.341 --> 00:04:17.291
even when you don't have

00:04:17.291 --> 00:04:19.979
a stronger motivation to do the work

00:04:19.979 --> 00:04:21.925
and so I would look at,
what are your big goals?

00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:23.751
What is it that you want to accomplish?

00:04:23.751 --> 00:04:26.270
How do you want to work on yourself?

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.331
And then look at your calendar

00:04:28.331 --> 00:04:32.034
and do the things in your
calendar mapped to your ambition?

00:04:32.034 --> 00:04:34.343
Are you doing the work
in your daily calendar,

00:04:34.343 --> 00:04:35.555
that will help you achieve your goals,

00:04:35.555 --> 00:04:38.489
because for too many people, it's not,

00:04:38.489 --> 00:04:39.742
too many have these big goals

00:04:39.742 --> 00:04:41.761
and then you look at how
they're spending their time,

00:04:41.761 --> 00:04:42.865
they don't match up,

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:44.070
the work they're doing consistently

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:46.364
is not going to help get
them to where they want to go

00:04:46.364 --> 00:04:48.171
and so those days of
inspiration are amazing,

00:04:48.171 --> 00:04:49.914
but if you want to continue the process

00:04:49.914 --> 00:04:51.581
and actually make real progress towards

00:04:51.581 --> 00:04:54.352
achieving your goals and
really building yourself up,

00:04:54.352 --> 00:04:55.984
then you have to schedule it in

00:04:55.984 --> 00:04:57.574
and make time for it to happen.

00:04:57.574 --> 00:05:00.253
So the question of the
day today is I'm curious,

00:05:00.253 --> 00:05:02.457
what is a daily non-negotiable for you?

00:05:02.457 --> 00:05:05.072
What do you schedule into
your calendar every day,

00:05:05.072 --> 00:05:07.305
at least during the week, weekdays,

00:05:07.305 --> 00:05:10.218
that has to happen every
day, non-negotiable,

00:05:10.218 --> 00:05:11.051
I'm curious to find out,

00:05:11.051 --> 00:05:12.314
leave it down in the Comments below.

00:05:12.314 --> 00:05:14.007
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:05:14.007 --> 00:05:17.041
to Margarita from
Lovesomi.com, thank you so much

00:05:17.041 --> 00:05:19.569
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word,

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:21.292
I really, really appreciate the support

00:05:21.292 --> 00:05:22.854
and I hope you enjoy the read.

00:05:22.854 --> 00:05:24.897
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:05:24.897 --> 00:05:25.730
I believe in you,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:27.912
I hope you can continue
to believe in yourself

00:05:27.912 --> 00:05:29.634
and whatever your one word is,

00:05:29.634 --> 00:05:31.550
I will see you again tomorrow morning

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:34.278
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:05:34.278 --> 00:05:36.519
♫ I wake up every morning

00:05:36.519 --> 00:05:39.849
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:42.886
(clicking typewriter keys)

00:05:42.886 --> 00:05:45.349
- I spoke at an education
summit for Microsoft,

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:49.182
I also spoke at an
education summit for Apple.

00:05:50.392 --> 00:05:52.290
At the education summit for Microsoft,

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:55.107
I would say that 70% of the executives

00:05:55.107 --> 00:05:58.040
spent about 70% of their presentations

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.707
talking about how to beat Apple.

00:06:02.504 --> 00:06:06.598
At the Apple education
summit, 100% of the executives

00:06:06.598 --> 00:06:09.206
spent 100% of their presentations

00:06:09.206 --> 00:06:10.800
talking about how to help teachers teach

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.372
and how to help students learn.

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:17.779
One is playing this way and
one is playing that way.

00:06:17.779 --> 00:06:21.019
One is playing finite and the
other one is playing infinite,

00:06:21.019 --> 00:06:23.904
guess which one gets frustrated?

00:06:23.904 --> 00:06:25.798
(audience laughing)

00:06:25.798 --> 00:06:27.526
So at the end of my talk at Microsoft,

00:06:27.526 --> 00:06:30.220
they gave me a gift, they
gave me the new Zune,

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.343
when it was a thing

00:06:31.343 --> 00:06:34.343
(audience laughing)

00:06:35.879 --> 00:06:39.724
and let me tell ya, this
thing was spectacular,

00:06:39.724 --> 00:06:42.676
it was the most elegant piece
of technology I'd ever used,

00:06:42.676 --> 00:06:45.343
the user interface was incredible,

00:06:45.343 --> 00:06:49.710
the design was spectacular,
I absolutely loved it,

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.241
it was easy to use and it
was bright and gorgeous and,

00:06:53.241 --> 00:06:55.326
it didn't work on Itunes,
which is a different problem,

00:06:55.326 --> 00:06:58.145
so I couldn't use it, but it was amazing

00:06:58.145 --> 00:06:59.925
(audience laughing)

00:06:59.925 --> 00:07:02.490
and elegant, my God, it was elegant.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.087
So I'm sitting in the back of a taxi

00:07:04.087 --> 00:07:05.499
with a very senior, Apple executive,

00:07:05.499 --> 00:07:08.332
sort of employee number 12 kind of guy

00:07:08.332 --> 00:07:11.031
and you know, I like to stir pots,

00:07:11.031 --> 00:07:14.439
so I turned to him and I said, "You know,

00:07:14.439 --> 00:07:17.999
"Microsoft gave me their new Zune

00:07:17.999 --> 00:07:21.999
"and it is so much better
than your Ipod Touch,"

00:07:23.299 --> 00:07:27.142
and he turned to me and he
said, "I have no doubt,"

00:07:27.142 --> 00:07:29.155
conversation over,

00:07:29.155 --> 00:07:32.155
(audience laughing)

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:35.342
because the infinite player understands,

00:07:35.342 --> 00:07:37.220
sometimes you're ahead and
sometimes you're behind,

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.376
sometimes your product is
better and sometimes its worse.

00:07:40.376 --> 00:07:43.087
The goal isn't to be the best every day,

00:07:43.087 --> 00:07:46.749
the goal isn't to outdo
your competition every day,

00:07:46.749 --> 00:07:48.814
that's a finite construction.

00:07:48.814 --> 00:07:50.470
If I had said to Microsoft,
"I've got the new Ipod Touch

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:52.271
"and it's so much better than your Zune,"

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:54.847
they would have said, "Can
we see it, what does it do?"

00:07:54.847 --> 00:07:57.322
react, react, react.

00:07:57.322 --> 00:08:02.143
Finite players play to beat
the people around them,

00:08:02.143 --> 00:08:06.720
infinite players play to
be better than themselves,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.111
to wake up every single day and say,

00:08:08.111 --> 00:08:10.430
"How can we make our
company a better version

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:12.960
"of itself today, than it was yesterday?

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.009
"How can we create a product this week,

00:08:15.009 --> 00:08:18.332
"that's better than the
product we created last week?"

00:08:18.332 --> 00:08:19.955
We also have to play the infinite game,

00:08:19.955 --> 00:08:21.634
it's not about being ranked number one,

00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:24.083
it's not about having
more followers on Twitter,

00:08:24.083 --> 00:08:27.209
than your friends, it's
not about outdoing anyone,

00:08:27.209 --> 00:08:29.948
it's about how to outdo yourself.

00:08:29.948 --> 00:08:31.748
It's not about selling more books

00:08:31.748 --> 00:08:34.206
or getting more TED
views than somebody else,

00:08:34.206 --> 00:08:35.235
it's about how to make sure

00:08:35.235 --> 00:08:37.000
that the work that you're producing

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.903
is better than the work
you produced before.

00:08:40.903 --> 00:08:42.810
You are your competition

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.804
and that is what ensures you
stay in the game the longest

00:08:45.804 --> 00:08:49.637
and that is what ensures you find joy,

00:08:49.637 --> 00:08:53.690
because the joy comes not from comparison,

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.389
but from advantage.

00:08:55.389 --> 00:08:57.305
- The concept of improving lives

00:08:57.305 --> 00:08:59.802
runs through the center of everything I do

00:08:59.802 --> 00:09:00.635
and then I realize

00:09:00.635 --> 00:09:03.052
that the way to improve lives

00:09:04.769 --> 00:09:08.271
is to continually improve yourself, right,

00:09:08.271 --> 00:09:13.031
so with that, every morning
when I get out of the bed,

00:09:13.031 --> 00:09:16.133
you know, I haven't fixed
everything in the world yet,

00:09:16.133 --> 00:09:18.696
so there's always something to do

00:09:18.696 --> 00:09:22.529
and in this film, I read
an interesting quote,

00:09:23.624 --> 00:09:27.707
the Siddhartha Gautama,
the Buddha, he said that,

00:09:28.978 --> 00:09:33.061
"Good people have to get
out of the bed every day

00:09:34.159 --> 00:09:38.452
"and try to empty the ocean with a ladle,"

00:09:38.452 --> 00:09:40.070
right, and I thought that was, you know,

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:43.235
I knew that was profound
and I paused for a second

00:09:43.235 --> 00:09:45.724
and I said, "Alright,
what the hell is a ladle?"

00:09:45.724 --> 00:09:47.906
alright.
(audience laughing)

00:09:47.906 --> 00:09:50.606
Right, so then, you know, I
just, I touched it on my Ipad,

00:09:50.606 --> 00:09:53.439
it was ladle, oh, it's like a
big spoon, a big spoon, okay,

00:09:53.439 --> 00:09:54.519
- [Host] As we say in Philly.
- it was like a soup spoon,

00:09:54.519 --> 00:09:56.134
yeah, (laughs) I was like a soup spoon,

00:09:56.134 --> 00:09:57.827
I was like, "Why a soup spoon?"

00:09:57.827 --> 00:10:02.558
so trying to empty the ocean
with a soup spoon, you know,

00:10:02.558 --> 00:10:05.642
as the mentality of how
you wake up every day

00:10:05.642 --> 00:10:07.851
to try to do good in the world,

00:10:07.851 --> 00:10:09.713
so for me,
- Yeah.

00:10:09.713 --> 00:10:12.963
I'm really driven by continually trying

00:10:16.739 --> 00:10:18.585
to elevate my mind

00:10:18.585 --> 00:10:21.858
and elevate my spirit and care for my body

00:10:21.858 --> 00:10:24.298
and to be able to love as many people

00:10:24.298 --> 00:10:26.465
as effectively as possible

